# Line endings are normalised to LF in the index and, deliberately, in the working tree too.
#
# The default on Windows (`core.autocrlf=true`) checks text files out as CRLF, which means the
# bytes a contributor edits are not the bytes committed. That is usually harmless. It is not
# harmless here, because the two backends emit `\n` unconditionally -- SvgWriter.fs: "line endings
# are always `\n`, never `Environment.NewLine`" -- and the golden baselines are their recorded
# output. `Golden.normalise` already defends the comparison on both sides, so this file is not
# fixing a bug; it stops the working tree and the repository from disagreeing about the contents
# of files whose whole purpose is to be compared as text and reviewed as a diff.
*               text=auto eol=lf

# Reviewable-as-text artefacts. Called out rather than left to `text=auto` so that a stray
# byte can never get one of them classified as binary, which would silently turn a golden-master
# diff into "Binary files differ" -- the one thing these formats were chosen to avoid.
*.svg           text eol=lf
*.tex           text eol=lf
*.dib           text eol=lf
*.fs            text eol=lf
*.fsx           text eol=lf
*.fsproj        text eol=lf
*.slnx          text eol=lf
*.props         text eol=lf
*.targets       text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf

# Genuinely binary.
*.png           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.nupkg         binary
# D62's stated face, under tests/Marey.Tests/fonts/. A font is the one binary in this tree a
# text-classifying heuristic might plausibly get wrong, and a mangled one fails as "Skia opens
# the file and the metrics are nonsense", which is a bad afternoon.
*.ttf           binary
*.otf           binary
